  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Bell Boeing Joint Project Office recorded $1.1B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date, across 1,041 awards with an average award size of $1M. Obligations fell 22.2% year over year. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $1B, while the General Services Administration obligated $2.1M. Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing is the largest NAICS market with $675M, followed by Aircraft Manufacturing with $333M. Fiscal-year obligations show a peak of $2.5B in FY21, a low of $871M in FY24, and $1.4B in FY25 before the current-year total of $1.1B. All figures are contract obligations except noted award-value figures in other tables, and current-year totals are partial fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How have obligations changed over the last five fiscal years?",
        "body": "Fiscal-year obligations moved from $2.5B in FY21 down to $1.9B in FY22 and then to $947M in FY23. Obligations were $871M in FY24, rose to $1.4B in FY25, and are $1.1B in FY26 to date. Obligations fell 22.2% year over year between the comparable reporting periods. These totals are partial for the current fiscal year."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and markets drive Bell Boeing Joint Project Office obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $1B and the General Services Administration obligated $2.1M in FY2026 to date. Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing accounted for $675M and Aircraft Manufacturing accounted for $333M of obligations. Smaller markets include Computer Systems Design Services at $35.2M and Engineering Services at $2.1M."
      }
    ]
  },
